  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 300 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nHighs topping out near 70 F for Thursday through Saturday:\n\nGlobal ensembles are in good agreement with a shortwave trough\nlifting north and west of the area Wednesday night. Previous \nforecast cycles had suggested a low-end chance for rain with \nthis system, but models have converged on keeping the surface \nfront and attendant synoptic lift to our north and west, keeping\nus all but dry. There still may be a very low chance for some \nstray light showers, but things look dry overall.\n\nDespite the traversal of the trough our geopotential heights\nwill only drop ever so slightly. The associated mass response\nfrom the surface low that will move through the northern Plains\nwill counteract these decreasing heights, bringing warmer and\nmoist air into the region for Thursday through Saturday, capping\nhighs out in the upper 60s to lower 70s. Better moisture will\nalso keep lows mild in the 40s and lower 50s.\n\nAs mentioned in previous forecast cycles, these temperature\nranges have remained high confidence with NBM interquartile\nspreads rather small. This continues to be the case with this\nforecast cycle.\n\n\n30-45% chance of rain on Saturday, though uncertainty remains:\n\nUncertainty continues to arise with seemingly any rain chances\nthat get presented to us. Unfortunately (as we continue to be in\nbad drought), rain chances for this weekend have dropped a bit\n(30-45% now). Discussed previously, the uncertainty with this\nnext system was both a timing and trough\nposition/amplitude/track issue. Latest ensemble model guidance\nhave pretty much converged on timing of Saturday or Saturday\nnight. There is still some uncertainty in the exact nature of\nthe shortwave, but clusters have also pointed to a similar\nprogression as the shortwave Wednesday with the axis of greatest\nlift staying west and north of our area. If this occurs, a\nsimilar drier trend may transpire in the coming days.\n\nClusters still differ on the amplitude of the trough, with the\nquarter of models showing a deeper trough pointing to a 30-40%\nchance of at least a tenth of an inch of rain with the system.\nHowever, with the other 75% of models looking at the broader\nlift staying north and west of the area, there is a 80-85%\nchance most areas see less than a tenth of an inch.\n\nThese chances are not helped by the fact that the rain chances\nare expected to be quick as a surface cold front rushes through\nthe area bringing temperatures back down to near normal for\nSunday.",
